(Preoperative intranasal dexmedetomidine reduces postoperative delirium and improves) Tj
T*
(recovery in elderly patients undergoing orthopedic surgery) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Abstract) Tj
T*
(Background: Elderly patients undergoing major orthopedic procedures face elevated risk for) Tj
T*
(postoperative delirium with significant morbidity implications. Objective: To determine) Tj
T*
(whether preoperative intranasal dexmedetomidine reduces delirium incidence in patients) Tj
T*
(aged 60+ undergoing hip or knee arthroplasty. Methods: A total of 232 patients were) Tj
T*
(randomly assigned to receive either dexmedetomidine \(100 µg\) or placebo intranasally) Tj
T*
(approximately 18 hours before surgery. Delirium assessment employed validated screening) Tj
T*
(instruments administered at 24, 48, and 72 hours post-op. Secondary measures included) Tj
T*
(sleep quality, anxiety, pain scores, and ambulation timing. Results: Delirium occurred in) Tj
T*
(11.2% of the dexmedetomidine group versus 23.3% in the placebo group \(relative risk 0.48;) Tj
T*
(95% CI 0.27-0.86; P = 0.019\). Sleep quality scores improved significantly with) Tj
T*
(pretreatment \(mean difference 2.8 points, P = 0.004\). Preoperative and postoperative) Tj
T*
(anxiety ratings were lower in the intervention arm \(P = 0.008\). Hemodynamic stability and) Tj
T*
(oxygenation remained stable in both groups. Conclusion: Preoperative intranasal) Tj
T*
(dexmedetomidine is effective and safe for delirium reduction in elderly joint surgery) Tj
T*
(patients, possibly through enhancement of preoperative sleep quality.) Tj
